metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-lines-2 = cons "## Whose shoulders" (cons "" (cons "Each person named in this section was the first on earth to make a fundamental move of intelligence: a move that computer science, technology and a healthy technological society stand on. The record proves each." (cons "" (cons "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was the first to cast reasoning as calculation in a universal formal language. In De Arte Combinatoria (1666), at twenty, he set out the program of a characteristic in which every concept has its exact sign and a calculus by which disputes are settled by computing. He was also the first to build a machine that performs all four operations of arithmetic: the stepped reckoner, shown to the Royal Society of London in 1673, whose stepped drum ran in calculating machines for two hundred years after. In 1703 he published the binary arithmetic every digital machine now computes in. His move: thought is computable." (cons "" (cons "Carl Adam Petri was the first to give concurrency a mathematics. His dissertation, Kommunikation mit Automaten (1962), modeled systems whose events happen independently, ordered only by what causes what, with no global clock, and every distributed system since is reasoned about in the terms he set. His move: many things act at once, and their order is their causality." (cons "" (cons "John McCarthy was the first to write a language whose evaluator is written in the language itself. In Recursive Functions of Symbolic Expressions and Their Computation by Machine (1960), Lisp's eval is a Lisp program; the same work introduced garbage collection, and McCarthy had already named the field of artificial intelligence in 1955. His move: a language can state its own meaning." (cons "" (cons "John Reynolds was the first to name and analyze that move. Definitional Interpreters for Higher-Order Programming Languages (1972) called such an interpreter metacircular, introduced defunctionalization, and showed how continuation-passing makes a defined language independent of the language that defines it. His move: the meaning of a language made exact through its own definition." (cons "" (cons "Brian Cantwell Smith was the first to build a program that steps up to reason about its own interpretation. His dissertation, Procedural Reflection in Programming Languages (1982), and its language 3-Lisp built the reflective tower. His move: a computation that can examine and change how it is being computed." (cons "" (cons "Vaughan Pratt was the first to model concurrency geometrically. In Modeling Concurrency with Geometry (1991) he introduced higher-dimensional automata: a process is a cubical set, two independent events span a square, three a cube, so concurrency is the dimension of the cube the events fill. Before it he founded dynamic logic (1976), the logic of what holds after a program runs; gave the first proof that every prime has a short certificate (1975); and modeled concurrency with partially ordered multisets (1986). With his group at Stanford he developed Chu spaces, where every object is a pairing of points with states, and from them the duality of time and information: events and states are two faces of one structure. His move: processes are cubes, and what happens and what is known are dual." (cons "" (cons "Vladimir Voevodsky was the first to make equivalence identity in a foundation of mathematics. His univalence axiom (formulated between 2006 and 2009) says that equivalent types are equal, so everything about one carries to the other, and his Univalent Foundations program, with the special year at the Institute for Advanced Study in 2012 and 2013, built mathematics a machine can check on that principle. He gave the rest of his life to it after a Fields Medal, and died in 2017 at fifty-one. His move: sameness is equivalence." (cons "" (cons "Cyril Cohen, Thierry Coquand, Simon Huber and Anders Mörtberg were the first to make univalence compute. Cubical Type Theory: a Constructive Interpretation of the Univalence Axiom (2015) gave a type theory in which transport along an equivalence is evaluation, building on the cubical model of Marc Bezem, Coquand and Huber. Their move: sameness is a computation." (cons "" (cons "Arthur Brock and Eric Harris-Braun were the first to design computing as receptors that carry their own meaning. The Ceptr Revelation (December 2010) set out a stack of receptors, lightweight machines that contain other receptors, exchanging data and protocols that describe themselves as semantic trees, with no central authority over addresses or names. Their move: meaning shared with no one owning it. Arthur is the person who set me on this path." (cons "" (cons "Paul Chiusano, Rúnar Bjarnason and Arya Irani were the first to identify every definition in a programming language by the hash of its syntax tree, with names kept as separate metadata. Unison, in development since 2013, made that the foundation of a language, its tooling and its distributed computing. Their move: code is what it is, wherever it is." (cons "" (cons "Jelle Herold, with Fabrizio Genovese and their team in Amsterdam, was the first to build a programming language on Petri nets and category theory: Statebox, set out in The Mathematical Specification of the Statebox Language (2019), where a program is a net and its runs are the arrows of the monoidal category the net generates. Their move: processes are mathematics that compose." (cons "" (cons "Each move is essential: take any one away and this work does not exist. Each of them holds all of the credit. Hyperactivity is their composition, and the composition is atomic, from the Greek *atomos*, uncut: one indivisible object with no seam along which it could be cut back into contributions. Each of their works, understood fully, is that whole; that is the univalent reading itself, where what is equivalent is the same. It is a universal singularity, the point where these lines of development converge and the field changes phase, and their work, among all the work of the field, is the core of its foundation for the rest of this century, a foundation whose mathematics and whose reach into technology outscale any prize the field gives. Hyperactivity takes no credit for any of these moves and inherits every claim they established: what each of them proved holds in the composition at its full strength. It is given away as a commons, open to everyone for good." (cons "" nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))
